{"sourceSha256": "0f12d0b03ecba8e951f6ea5aac3cf3f22af0fd1a0691fadeae30c78d5db3ed68", "text": "af 21% @ 5:44 PM\n\n&\n\nQ\n\nTicket Id: 23547\n\n15 July 2017\n\nNurse-in-Charge\n\nDistrict Nursing Service\nGreenville Health Care Centre\n$8 Highton Road\n\nGreenville\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\nRe: Ms. Margaret Helen Martin, 0.0.8125 July 1935\n\nlam writing to refer Ms. Martin, an 81 yeareold single woman who was diagnosed with coronary artery\ndisease and had been experiencing angina. Due to her condition, she underwent angioplasty and was\nadvised by the cardiologist to be discharged tomorrow. It would be greatly appreciated if you could help\nher with the follow-up health care management\n\nHer medical history revealed that her initial angioplasty happened in 2008. Since then, she has been taking\nantihypertensive drugs: aspirin and clopidogrel (Plavix) for her coronary artery disease, metoprolol\n(Betaloc) and ramipril (Tritace) to normalize her blood pressure, and atorvastatin (Lipitor) to control her\ncholesterol level,\n\nAfter her surgery, she was routinely monitored for the recovery Nursing assessment showed bruising at\nthe catheterization site but no signs of infection nor bleeding, Today, Ms. Martin can tolerate fluids and\nlight meals; however, she is anxious about returning home because she is in doubt of her coping capability.\n\nUpon discharge, it is necessary to monitor for further bruising or possible infection on the operative site\nAlso, strict monitoring of her medication regime is emphasized. Please arrange regular family visits to\n\nmonitor her progress.\nYours sincerely,\nKrizel Angela A. Castro\n\nWard Nurse\nGreenville Public\n\nHospital\n\nWord length 186\n\nComments This is a very good letter with appropriate\nselection of case notes and logical paragraphing.\nThere is good coherence throughout. There are\nonly minor inaccuracies and the letter meets the\nexpectations.\nEstimated Grade Be\nAdvice 1. Revise grammar\n2. Always proofread your letter\n3. Keep up the good work!\nHelpful links hitos//www. benchmarkedy.com_au/oet-reading\n\nprattice-tests\nhitos//www benchmarkedu.com_au/oet-writing-\n\ncorrection\n\nhitos://www.facebook.com/groups/oethelp\n\n296 He\n\nCommented (ESL1):\n\nCommented (E512): hai been", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "af 21% @ 5:44 PM\n\n&\n\nQ\n\nTicket Id: 23547\n\n15 July 2017\n\nNurse-in-Charge\n\nDistrict Nursing Service\nGreenville Health Care Centre\n$8 Highton Road\n\nGreenville\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\nRe: Ms. Margaret Helen Martin, 0.0.8125 July 1935\n\nlam writing to refer Ms. Martin, an 81 yeareold single woman who was diagnosed with coronary artery\ndisease and had been experiencing angina. Due to her condition, she underwent angioplasty and was\nadvised by the cardiologist to be discharged tomorrow. It would be greatly appreciated if you could help\nher with the follow-up health care management\n\nHer medical history revealed that her initial angioplasty happened in 2008. Since then, she has been taking\nantihypertensive drugs: aspirin and clopidogrel (Plavix) for her coronary artery disease, metoprolol\n(Betaloc) and ramipril (Tritace) to normalize her blood pressure, and atorvastatin (Lipitor) to control her\ncholesterol level,\n\nAfter her surgery, she was routinely monitored for the recovery Nursing assessment showed bruising at\nthe catheterization site but no signs of infection nor bleeding, Today, Ms. Martin can tolerate fluids and\nlight meals; however, she is anxious about returning home because she is in doubt of her coping capability.\n\nUpon discharge, it is necessary to monitor for further bruising or possible infection on the operative site\nAlso, strict monitoring of her medication regime is emphasized. Please arrange regular family visits to\n\nmonitor her progress.\nYours sincerely,\nKrizel Angela A. Castro\n\nWard Nurse\nGreenville Public\n\nHospital\n\nWord length 186\n\nComments This is a very good letter with appropriate\nselection of case notes and log